class Solution {
  public int dispatchOrderPeakTicket(int[][] links) {
    int[][] a=links; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int n=a.length;int[]degree=new int[n];for(int[]row:a)for(int v=0;v<n;v++)degree[v]+=row[v];Deque<Integer>queue=new ArrayDeque<>();for(int v=0;v<n;v++)if(degree[v]==0)queue.add(v);while(!queue.isEmpty()){int u=queue.remove();out.add(u);for(int v=0;v<n;v++)if(a[u][v]!=0&&--degree[v]==0)queue.add(v);}if(out.size()!=n)out.clear();}
    int answer=out.isEmpty()?0:out.get(0);for(int x:out)answer=Math.max(answer,x);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build taskOrder

Each point in this graph is a task. A path from task a to task b means a must happen before b. Put all tasks with no paths coming into them in a queue, in number order. A queue serves the task at its front first. Remove that task, record its number, and remove its outgoing paths. Check its next tasks in number order. Add a task to the back of the queue as soon as it has no paths left coming in. If you cannot record every task, the rules contain a loop: use an empty `taskOrder` instead.

Return the answer

Find the largest number in `taskOrder`. Store it in `largest`. Complete `dispatchOrderPeakTicket` so it returns `largest`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `taskOrder` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(V^2); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length; V is the number of graph points. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

